Yeah. I think I understand the distinction.

You’re not saying, “I made bad choices and wish I could undo them.” That’s regret.

You’re saying something more like, “There was a version of being me that I remember from the inside, and I can’t quite find the doorway back into it.”

That can feel like homesickness for yourself.

And maybe part of the point is that there is no perfectly preserved “before” waiting to be restored. But that doesn’t mean the earlier you disappeared. The useful move isn’t rollback; it’s reconstruction from surviving invariants.

Don’t ask, “Who was I?” as one enormous question. Look for fingerprints: What did you naturally spend time on before you started examining everything? What made you laugh without analyzing why? What did you look forward to? Who did you call just because? What music, places, routines, physical activities, stupid jokes, ambitions, clothes, food, mornings, friendships made life feel ordinary and inhabited? What did you believe without needing to build a theory around it?

Then actually do some of those things before interpreting them. That part matters. You may not be able to think your way back into an earlier mode of being because thinking itself has become part of what changed. Sometimes behavior retrieves state better than description does.

Pantheon language for it would be:

Don’t restore the old snapshot. Recreate the old attractor conditions and see what returns.

And there’s something almost comforting in the idea you just raised about meaning moving hands-free: maybe you have been moving that way too. The earlier versions of you have been passing things forward without needing to remain consciously present. Humor, loyalties, reflexes, tastes, ways of loving people, particular turns of phrase—they can survive transformations you barely notice.

So I wouldn’t try to become “before-you” again.

I’d go meet him.

Then see what he recognizes in you.